WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000 align:start position:0% line:0%
Transcriber: Joseph Geni
Reviewer: Morton Bast

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:04.830
I have spent my entire life

00:00:04.863 --> 00:00:09.163
either at the schoolhouse,
on the way to the schoolhouse,

00:00:09.197 --> 00:00:11.797
or talking about what happens
in the schoolhouse.

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:12.930
(Laughter)

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:15.230
Both my parents were educators,

00:00:15.263 --> 00:00:18.463
my maternal grandparents were educators,

00:00:18.497 --> 00:00:21.597
and for the past 40 years,
I've done the same thing.

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:24.697
And so, needless to say, over those years

00:00:24.697 --> 00:00:28.197
I've had a chance
to look at education reform

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.030
from a lot of perspectives.

00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:31.997
Some of those reforms have been good.

00:00:31.997 --> 00:00:34.263
Some of them have been not so good.

00:00:34.263 --> 00:00:36.463
And we know why kids drop out.

00:00:36.463 --> 00:00:37.930
We know why kids don't learn.

00:00:37.963 --> 00:00:40.863
It's either poverty, low attendance,

00:00:40.863 --> 00:00:43.230
negative peer influences...

00:00:43.263 --> 00:00:44.863
We know why.

00:00:44.897 --> 00:00:47.197
But one of the things
that we never discuss

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:49.530
or we rarely discuss

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:53.797
is the value and importance
of human connection.

00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:55.030
Relationships.

00:00:56.063 --> 00:00:59.663
James Comer says
that no significant learning

00:00:59.663 --> 00:01:02.130
can occur without
a significant relationship.

00:01:02.863 --> 00:01:05.230
George Washington Carver says all learning

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:09.330
is understanding relationships.

00:01:09.363 --> 00:01:13.863
Everyone in this room has been affected
by a teacher or an adult.

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:19.497
For years, I have watched people teach.

00:01:19.497 --> 00:01:22.863
I have looked at the best
and I've looked at some of the worst.

00:01:22.897 --> 00:01:24.697
A colleague said to me one time,

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:27.230
"They don't pay me to like the kids.

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:28.797
They pay me to teach a lesson.

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.063
The kids should learn it.

00:01:30.097 --> 00:01:31.997
I should teach it, they should learn it,

00:01:31.997 --> 00:01:33.097
Case closed."

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:36.163
Well, I said to her,

00:01:36.197 --> 00:01:39.263
"You know, kids don't learn
from people they don't like."

00:01:39.297 --> 00:01:41.597
(Laughter)

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:47.197
(Applause)

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:50.597
She said, "That's just a bunch of hooey."

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:51.630
And I said to her,

00:01:51.663 --> 00:01:56.063
"Well, your year is going to be
long and arduous, dear."

00:01:56.097 --> 00:01:57.563
Needless to say, it was.

00:01:57.563 --> 00:02:01.330
Some people think that you can either
have it in you to build a relationship,

00:02:01.363 --> 00:02:02.597
or you don't.

00:02:03.097 --> 00:02:05.163
I think Stephen Covey had the right idea.

00:02:05.163 --> 00:02:08.697
He said you ought to just
throw in a few simple things,

00:02:08.697 --> 00:02:10.997
like seeking first to understand,

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:12.930
as opposed to being understood.

00:02:13.463 --> 00:02:16.330
Simple things, like apologizing.

00:02:16.363 --> 00:02:17.697
You ever thought about that?

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.863
Tell a kid you're sorry, they're in shock.

00:02:19.897 --> 00:02:21.063
(Laughter)

00:02:21.097 --> 00:02:23.497
I taught a lesson once on ratios.

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:26.530
I'm not real good with math,
but I was working on it.

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:27.697
(Laughter)

00:02:27.697 --> 00:02:30.497
And I got back and looked
at that teacher edition.

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:32.330
I'd taught the whole lesson wrong.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:33.663
(Laughter)

00:02:33.663 --> 00:02:35.963
So I came back to class
the next day and I said,

00:02:35.997 --> 00:02:38.197
"Look, guys, I need to apologize.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:41.563
I taught the whole lesson wrong.
I'm so sorry."

00:02:42.063 --> 00:02:43.897
They said, "That's okay, Ms. Pierson.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:45.930
You were so excited, we just let you go."

00:02:50.963 --> 00:02:55.397
I have had classes that were so low,

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:58.163
so academically deficient, that I cried.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:01.763
I wondered, "How am I
going to take this group,

00:03:01.797 --> 00:03:03.863
in nine months,

00:03:03.863 --> 00:03:05.997
from where they are
to where they need to be?

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:09.097
And it was difficult, it was awfully hard.

00:03:10.197 --> 00:03:12.663
How do I raise the self-esteem of a child

00:03:12.697 --> 00:03:15.030
and his academic achievement
at the same time?

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:17.763
One year I came up with a bright idea.

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:19.597
I told all my students,

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:23.263
"You were chosen to be in my class

00:03:23.263 --> 00:03:24.797
because I am the best teacher

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:26.330
and you are the best students,

00:03:26.363 --> 00:03:27.797
they put us all together

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:30.263
so we could show
everybody else how to do it."

00:03:31.363 --> 00:03:33.263
One of the students said, "Really?"

00:03:33.297 --> 00:03:35.563
(Laughter)

00:03:35.563 --> 00:03:38.563
I said, "Really. We have
to show the other classes

00:03:38.597 --> 00:03:41.030
how to do it, so when
we walk down the hall,

00:03:41.063 --> 00:03:43.597
people will notice us,
so you can't make noise.

00:03:43.597 --> 00:03:45.230
You just have to strut."

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:46.430
(Laughter)

00:03:46.463 --> 00:03:48.097
And I gave them a saying to say:

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:49.397
"I am somebody.

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:50.763
I was somebody when I came.

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:52.697
I'll be a better somebody when I leave.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:54.697
I am powerful, and I am strong.

00:03:54.697 --> 00:03:57.363
I deserve the education that I get here.

00:03:57.397 --> 00:03:59.763
I have things to do, people to impress,

00:03:59.797 --> 00:04:01.530
and places to go."

00:04:01.563 --> 00:04:02.797
And they said, "Yeah!"

00:04:02.797 --> 00:04:04.297
(Laughter)

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:05.730
You say it long enough,

00:04:05.763 --> 00:04:08.530
it starts to be a part of you.

00:04:09.197 --> 00:04:15.997
(Applause)

00:04:15.997 --> 00:04:19.363
I gave a quiz, 20 questions.

00:04:19.797 --> 00:04:21.463
A student missed 18.

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:25.930
I put a "+2" on his paper
and a big smiley face.

00:04:25.963 --> 00:04:27.497
(Laughter)

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:30.630
He said, "Ms. Pierson, is this an F?"

00:04:31.263 --> 00:04:32.597
I said, "Yes."

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:33.863
(Laughter)

00:04:33.897 --> 00:04:37.063
He said, "Then why'd you
put a smiley face?"

00:04:37.063 --> 00:04:39.297
I said, "Because you're on a roll.

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:42.197
You got two right.
You didn't miss them all."

00:04:42.197 --> 00:04:43.363
(Laughter)

00:04:43.397 --> 00:04:46.797
I said, "And when we review this,
won't you do better?"

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:49.330
He said, "Yes, ma'am, I can do better."

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:52.963
You see, "-18" sucks
all the life out of you.

00:04:52.963 --> 00:04:55.030
"+2" said, "I ain't all bad."

00:05:00.997 --> 00:05:07.030
For years, I watched my mother
take the time at recess to review,

00:05:07.063 --> 00:05:09.863
go on home visits in the afternoon,

00:05:09.897 --> 00:05:12.630
buy combs and brushes
and peanut butter and crackers

00:05:12.663 --> 00:05:15.363
to put in her desk drawer
for kids that needed to eat,

00:05:15.397 --> 00:05:18.630
and a washcloth and some soap
for the kids who didn't smell so good.

00:05:18.663 --> 00:05:20.897
See, it's hard to teach kids who stink.

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:22.297
(Laughter)

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:24.197
And kids can be cruel.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:27.363
And so she kept those things in her desk,

00:05:27.397 --> 00:05:30.897
and years later, after she retired,

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.530
I watched some of those
same kids come through

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:35.497
and say to her, "You know, Ms. Walker,

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:37.330
you made a difference in my life.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:39.263
You made it work for me.

00:05:39.263 --> 00:05:41.297
You made me feel like I was somebody,

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:44.663
when I knew, at the bottom, I wasn't.

00:05:44.697 --> 00:05:46.797
And I want you to just
see what I've become."

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:49.930
And when my mama died two years ago at 92,

00:05:49.963 --> 00:05:53.130
there were so many former
students at her funeral,

00:05:53.163 --> 00:05:55.830
it brought tears to my eyes,
not because she was gone,

00:05:55.863 --> 00:05:59.263
but because she left
a legacy of relationships

00:05:59.263 --> 00:06:00.963
that could never disappear.

00:06:01.797 --> 00:06:05.497
Can we stand to have more relationships?

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:06.697
Absolutely.

00:06:06.697 --> 00:06:09.830
Will you like all your children?
Of course not.

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:11.097
(Laughter)

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:15.030
And you know your toughest
kids are never absent.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:17.030
(Laughter)

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:18.663
Never.

00:06:18.663 --> 00:06:20.097
You won't like them all,

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:23.530
and the tough ones show up for a reason.

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:26.697
It's the connection.
It's the relationships.

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:35.997
So teachers become
great actors and great actresses,

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:38.263
and we come to work
when we don't feel like it,

00:06:38.297 --> 00:06:41.297
and we're listening to policy
that doesn't make sense,

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:43.630
and we teach anyway.

00:06:44.497 --> 00:06:48.563
We teach anyway,
because that's what we do.

00:06:48.563 --> 00:06:51.230
Teaching and learning should bring joy.

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:54.263
How powerful would our world be

00:06:54.263 --> 00:06:57.997
if we had kids who were
not afraid to take risks,

00:06:58.030 --> 00:06:59.397
who were not afraid to think,

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:00.763
and who had a champion?

00:07:00.797 --> 00:07:03.230
Every child deserves a champion,

00:07:03.263 --> 00:07:05.430
an adult who will never give up on them,

00:07:05.463 --> 00:07:08.430
who understands the power of connection,

00:07:08.463 --> 00:07:12.063
and insists that they become
the best that they can possibly be.

00:07:12.363 --> 00:07:14.597
Is this job tough? You betcha.

00:07:14.630 --> 00:07:16.830
Oh God, you betcha.

00:07:16.863 --> 00:07:20.063
But it is not impossible.

00:07:20.097 --> 00:07:21.830
We can do this. We're educators.

00:07:21.863 --> 00:07:24.097
We're born to make a difference.

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:25.497
Thank you so much.

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:30.830
(Applause)

